## What counts as content

- `messages/*.json`: UI strings, tool descriptions, FAQ questions and
  answers, and marketing copy, in every language
- `lib/tool-faqs.ts`: FAQ content definitions
- `lib/guides/*.ts`: the how-to guides
- `lib/comparisons/*.ts`: the comparison pages
- `lib/upload-limits/{en,fr,ar}.ts`: the prose of the upload limits page
- The prose of any page under `app/[locale]/**` (headings, body text, FAQ
  text, comparison copy) and of any blog post
- `public/llms.txt` and `public/llms-full.txt`
- The screenshots and test outputs in `public/guides/` and `public/compare/`

The surrounding code (components, layout, routing, schema generation, the
PDF pipeline and the i18n plumbing) is AGPL-3.0. The upload limits table itself
(`lib/upload-limits/data.ts`, also served as `/pdf-upload-limits.csv` and
`.json`) and the Arabic PDF editing test set (`public/arabic-pdf-editor-test/`,
its cases and results) are published under CC BY 4.0, as their pages say. The fonts in `public/fonts/` keep
their own licences, which sit next to each file.

